; smf-smpte — a wall-clock division, and the tempo event nobody believes.
;
; 25 frames per second, 40 ticks per frame: OpenMPT's `frames ·
; subframes / 2` makes that a 500-pulse grid, and the file's `set-tempo`
; is kept and recorded as ignored (`smpte_tempo_ignored`, plan 03).
;
; This is one of the corpus's two NON-CANONICAL fixtures, and it is here
; for rung 2. The canonical writer's division is ALWAYS pulses per
; quarter (plan 05, "The canonical writer"), so `write(read(bytes))`
; differs from these bytes in exactly the two division bytes and
; `(rung1 :ok false)` is the expected, PINNED fact — rung 1 is a gate on
; files the writer produced, and this file is authored to be one it never
; would. Rung 2 is what the case asserts: every event comes back with its
; tick, its kind and its payload after the file has been round-tripped
; onto the grid its division converted to, which is plan 09's rung for a
; foreign file. A golden is reviewed, not obeyed: the `:ok false` here is
; read as "the writer normalised it, on purpose", and the byte-equality
; of this whole text with `expected.sjon` is the gate.
;
; `(write)` beside it hashes that normalised output, so a change in what
; the writer makes of a SMPTE file moves a number rather than a verdict.
(read)
(roundtrip)
(write)
